partialText:
&lt;p&gt;VoiceDash is the client portal I build for voice AI agencies: each agency's clients log in and see their own agent's calls, transcripts and analytics. For a long time those calls arrived two ways. Retell pushed them by webhook, and there was a "Sync Calls" button for everything the webhook missed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The button was the problem. Nobody presses a sync button on a dashboard they opened to read numbers. So I wrote a small React hook that syncs on its own, shipped it, and less than an hour later shipped a fix for a case I had not thought about: a tab you have not looked at yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is that hook, the one-line bug, and the parts I still have not fixed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the hook does
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two client pages show calls: the conversation list and the analytics dashboard. Both now call the same hook with the agent's ID and a callback that reloads the page's data.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;SYNC_EVERY_MS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/** Returning to the tab syncs again unless the last sync was this recent. */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;MIN_GAP_ON_RETURN_MS&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;15_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;agentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSynced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSyncedRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;onSynced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSyncedRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSynced&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;agentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;stopped&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lastRun&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// sync(), onVisibilityChange(), the timer and the cleanup go here&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;agentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Three triggers: when the page opens, when the tab comes back into view (unless it synced in the last 15 seconds), and every minute while the tab is visible. The callback lives in a ref so the effect does not tear down and restart its timer every time the parent re-renders with a new arrow function. The effect depends only on &lt;code&gt;agentId&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;code&gt;running&lt;/code&gt; flag stops a slow sync from overlapping with the next tick. &lt;code&gt;stopped&lt;/code&gt; stops a sync that finishes after the user navigated away from calling back into an unmounted page.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why "only while visible"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The server route behind the hook calls Retell's list-calls API with the agent's key. A dashboard left open in a forgotten tab for a weekend should not call a vendor API 2,880 times for nobody. So the first version guarded every sync the same way:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;visibilityState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visible"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;setInterval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;SYNC_EVERY_MS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That reads fine. It is also wrong for one case that showed up almost immediately.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The background tab
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open a dashboard with a middle click. Or restore a browser session with five portal tabs. The page loads, renders the calls already in the database, and its &lt;code&gt;visibilityState&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;"hidden"&lt;/code&gt;. The opening sync returns early.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Nothing is broken yet. The moment you switch to that tab, &lt;code&gt;visibilitychange&lt;/code&gt; fires and the hook syncs. But between load and switch, the page shows stale data while looking finished, and the dashboard's whole promise was "you never press Sync".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The fix was to treat the opening sync differently from the repeat:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;evenIfHidden&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;evenIfHidden&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;visibilityState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"visible"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;lastRun&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;now&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;fetch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/api/retell/sync-calls"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"POST"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;headers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"Content-Type"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"application/json"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="n"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;stringify&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;agentId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Calls that arrived by webhook still show; the next sync retries.&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;finally&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;running&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stopped&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;onSyncedRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;evenIfHidden&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;setInterval&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;sync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="no"&gt;SYNC_EVERY_MS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Note the &lt;code&gt;() =&amp;gt; sync()&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;code&gt;setInterval&lt;/code&gt;. Passing &lt;code&gt;sync&lt;/code&gt; directly would still work here because &lt;code&gt;setInterval&lt;/code&gt; calls it with no arguments, but the wrapper makes it obvious that the repeat never gets &lt;code&gt;evenIfHidden&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule I took from it: a page someone opened is a request, even if they are not looking at it yet. A timer someone forgot about is not. Those deserve different guards, and I had written one guard for both.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Refreshing without flicker
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Syncing is half of it. After a sync the page has to show the result without becoming a loading skeleton every minute.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On analytics, the fetch takes a &lt;code&gt;quiet&lt;/code&gt; flag that skips the loading placeholders, plus a request counter so an old response cannot overwrite a newer date range the user picked meanwhile:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fetchData&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;quiet&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;latestRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;quiet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setLoading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// ... fetch /api/analytics&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;ok&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;latestRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;setData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="nf"&gt;useCallSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;agentId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;fetchData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The conversation list was harder because the user might have scrolled through three pages and opened a transcript. Replacing the list would throw that away. So the refresh fetches the newest page and merges: unseen calls go on top, calls already shown get their fields updated (a call stored mid-call gets its final duration and transcript later), and the list is re-sorted by start time. If the search box changed while the request was in flight, the result belongs to a different list and is dropped.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What makes it safe to call this often
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The client side is the easy part. The reason a once-a-minute sync is cheap lives on the server, and I covered most of it in &lt;a href="https://dev.to/nabeelbaghoor/a-webhook-tells-you-something-happened-not-that-you-have-everything-1fck"&gt;A Webhook Tells You Something Happened, Not That You Have Everything&lt;/a&gt;. The short version:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Each agent stores &lt;code&gt;callsSyncedUntil&lt;/code&gt;, how far its Retell history is known to be complete. A routine sync reads oldest first from two hours before that point, so it is usually a single request.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The watermark only moves forward. The update is conditional (&lt;code&gt;callsSyncedUntil: null&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;lt: until&lt;/code&gt;), so two syncs of the same agent racing each other cannot move it backward.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Inserts use &lt;code&gt;createMany&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;skipDuplicates&lt;/code&gt; against a unique key on &lt;code&gt;(agentId, platformCallId)&lt;/code&gt;. Two tabs syncing the same agent at the same second store each call once.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The route spends at most 25 seconds answering, then hands whatever is left of a long history to Next.js &lt;code&gt;after()&lt;/code&gt; with a bigger budget and a resume cursor.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Without those four, a polling hook on the client would be a load generator. With them, the hook is mostly a trigger.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I have not fixed
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Honest list, from reading the code again for this post. None of these have shipped.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The hook ignores the response status.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;fetch&lt;/code&gt; only rejects on network failure. If the route returns 502 because Retell is down, or 400 because the key was removed, the hook treats it as a finished sync, calls &lt;code&gt;onSynced&lt;/code&gt;, and tries again in a minute. No backoff, and nothing on screen says the numbers are no longer being refreshed. The page just quietly stops changing. I would rather show a small "last synced 14 minutes ago" line than have someone trust a frozen dashboard.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Every visible tab polls on its own.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;running&lt;/code&gt; is per hook instance. Two tabs on the same agent's analytics, both visible on two monitors, make two sync requests a minute. The server makes that safe, not free. A &lt;code&gt;BroadcastChannel&lt;/code&gt; so one tab syncs and the others just refresh would fix it. I have not needed it badly enough yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The list merge only looks at the newest page.&lt;/strong&gt; If a sync backfills calls from last month (a gap left by a broken webhook, say), they land in the database but not in a list you already scrolled, because the merge only fetches the first 50. They appear on the next full load. Rare, but it is exactly the case the backfill exists for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A first sync can hold the request for 25 seconds.&lt;/strong&gt; For an agent with no watermark, the opening sync reads Retell history up to the route's budget before answering. The page is usable the whole time, but the "new calls appear" moment can lag on a freshly connected agent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I would tell myself before writing it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Polling from the browser is a reasonable choice when the server work is idempotent and incremental. Most of my effort went there, not into the hook, and that was the right split.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hook itself needed exactly one more distinction than I gave it: who asked for this sync? The page opening, the user coming back, or a timer. Each one deserves its own guard, and collapsing them into a single &lt;code&gt;visibilityState&lt;/code&gt; check cost me a release.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you build portals for voice agents, this is part of what goes into &lt;a href="https://voice-dash.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;VoiceDash&lt;/a&gt;. And if you have solved the "one tab syncs, the rest listen" problem in production, I would like to know whether &lt;code&gt;BroadcastChannel&lt;/code&gt; held up or whether you ended up with locks in &lt;code&gt;localStorage&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On paper, building a resume editor sounds like a weekend project. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You throw together a two-column layout: form inputs on the left, a live preview on the right, and a shiny "Download PDF" button up top. Simple, right?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Except it almost never is. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The moment a user actually relies on your tool to apply for a job, expectations skyrocket. They expect the exported file to mirror the on-screen preview pixel for pixel. They expect it to fit neatly onto standard A4 paper without awkward blank gaps. They want the typography intact. And most importantly: they expect the output to be a &lt;em&gt;living, breathing document&lt;/em&gt;—one with selectable, copyable text that applicant tracking systems (ATS) can actually parse, and maybe even a Word (.docx) file they can tweak later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While building &lt;strong&gt;Pagewise&lt;/strong&gt;, I quickly realized a fundamental truth that reshaped how I approached the entire architecture:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A document that looks great in a web browser isn’t guaranteed to survive printing, PDF parsing, or external word processors.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most web tools take the easy way out and treat resumes like glorified screenshots. We wanted Pagewise to treat a resume as a real document. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is how we tackled the engineering headaches behind making that happen.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  1. The A4 Problem: A Browser Is Not a Sheet of Paper
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Web browsers are built for fluid, responsive layouts. Documents are the exact opposite they are strictly bound by physical dimensions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An A4 page is strictly:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Width:&lt;/strong&gt; 210 mm&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Height:&lt;/strong&gt; 297 mm&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you design a document editor purely using pixels or viewport percentages, you set yourself up for heartbreak. A layout might look gorgeous on a 1440p monitor, but the second the user tries to print or export it, things fall apart:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Browser print drivers apply arbitrary scaling.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Default margins eat away at your layout.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Sections awkwardly break right across the middle of a job title.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Content spills over onto a practically empty second page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To solve this, we had to stop treating the canvas like a responsive webpage and start treating it as a physical medium. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our CSS establishes a rigid document boundary matching real-world paper dimensions:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;.resume-page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;210mm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;min-height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;297mm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;box-sizing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;border-box&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;position&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;relative&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nl"&gt;background&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mh"&gt;#ffffff&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;print&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;@page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;size&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;A4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;margin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nc"&gt;.resume-page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;210mm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;min-height&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;297mm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;margin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;box-shadow&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;none&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Note: This is a simplified snippet, but it highlights the core mindset shift.&lt;/em&gt; &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Of course, CSS dimensions alone don’t magically solve pagination. When a user writes a lengthy work history, handling dynamic overflow without severing bullet points mid-sentence requires active content measurement and calculated page breaks.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Typography Is Document Geometry, Not Just Decoration
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One of the sneakiest bugs we ran into had nothing to do with margins or flexbox it was fonts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In web design, a font change is mostly cosmetic. In fixed-geometry document design, &lt;strong&gt;fonts dictate layout math.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A subtle change in font weight, letter spacing, or line height can:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Alter exact line-wrapping points.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Expand a 2-line bullet point into 3 lines.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Increase section height by just 4 pixels... pushing an entire section onto an unwanted second page.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This becomes a nightmare when your frontend preview uses local system fonts or Google Fonts, while your backend PDF engine runs on a Linux server that has neither. When the server falls back to standard Helvetica or DejaVu Sans, your carefully calibrated single-page resume suddenly bleeds into page two.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  Our Font Normalization Matrix
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To combat this, we set up a controlled normalization strategy. Whenever a user picks a common font, our pipeline pairs it with verified, metrically close open-source equivalents across environments:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Requested Font&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Normalized / Open-Source Equivalent&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Georgia&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Lora / Liberation Serif&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Arial&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Arimo&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Calibri&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Carlito&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Are they 100% identical down to the sub-pixel kerning pair in every environment? No. But this controlled mapping drastically reduces surprise layout shifts. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The takeaway:&lt;/strong&gt; If typography shifts between preview and export, your document geometry breaks. Font consistency must be treated as an infrastructure problem.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  3. The "Canvas Trap": Why We Refused to Flatten to Images
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you look at how many modern web apps generate PDFs, you'll see a very common shortcut:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Run &lt;code&gt;html2canvas&lt;/code&gt; to take a screenshot of the DOM.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Slap that raster image inside a &lt;code&gt;jsPDF&lt;/code&gt; wrapper.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Call it a day.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It looks identical to the preview! It’s fast to build! &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;And it produces a terrible resume.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you turn a document into a flat picture, you destroy its utility:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Screenshot / Canvas PDF&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;True Vector &amp;amp; Text PDF&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Text is baked into flat pixels&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Text remains sharp, selectable, and highlightable&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Copy-pasting text is impossible&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Recruiters can easily copy contact info or bullet points&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ATS parsers fail or require messy OCR&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ATS software reads native text streams directly&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Blurry when zoomed in or printed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Crisp vector rendering at any scale&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Zero screen-reader accessibility&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Screen-reader friendly&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Imagine a recruiter trying to copy your email or phone number from a resume, only to find they're clicking on a JPEG. Or worse, an automated ATS pipeline rejecting your application because it couldn't extract a single keyword from an image.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;At Pagewise, we committed to &lt;strong&gt;true vector text&lt;/strong&gt;. When you download a PDF, the text remains real, selectable text. While no format can guarantee 100% compatibility across every single outdated ATS parser in existence, outputting real text gives candidates the best possible shot.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  4. One Data Model, Two Completely Different Export Paths
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;People don’t just want PDFs. A huge chunk of users still need an editable Microsoft Word (&lt;code&gt;.docx&lt;/code&gt;) file—whether to submit through job portals that require it or to make custom adjustments for a specific application.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The naive solution would be trying to convert the rendered HTML into Word, or exporting an image into a DOCX file. Both create a mess.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead, we decoupled our &lt;strong&gt;document state&lt;/strong&gt; from our &lt;strong&gt;rendering targets&lt;/strong&gt;. A single JSON resume model feeds into an export dispatcher with two dedicated pipelines:&lt;br&gt;
              ┌────────────────────────┐&lt;br&gt;
              │  Resume Document Model │&lt;br&gt;
              └───────────┬────────────┘&lt;br&gt;
                          │&lt;br&gt;
                  Export Dispatcher&lt;br&gt;
                         / \&lt;br&gt;
                        /   \&lt;br&gt;
                       v     v&lt;br&gt;
          ┌─────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────┐&lt;br&gt;
          │ Visual Pipeline │ │   python-docx   │&lt;br&gt;
          │  (Vector PDF)   │ │  (XML-level)    │&lt;br&gt;
          └────────┬────────┘ └────────┬────────┘&lt;br&gt;
                   │                   │&lt;br&gt;
                   v                   v&lt;br&gt;
          ┌─────────────────┐ ┌─────────────────┐&lt;br&gt;
          │ Sharp, ATS-ready│ │ Native Editable │&lt;br&gt;
          │   PDF Document  │ │   Word Styles   │&lt;br&gt;
          └─────────────────┘ └─────────────────┘&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Path A (Visual PDF):&lt;/strong&gt; Renders the document model through a headless browser environment configured with strict print stylesheets, generating real text, vector borders, and predictable page breaks.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Path B (Native DOCX):&lt;/strong&gt; Feeds the raw structured data into &lt;code&gt;python-docx&lt;/code&gt;. Instead of hacking HTML tags into Word, it builds real Word paragraphs, native headings, real bullet lists, and genuine XML table structures.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This means when a user opens the &lt;code&gt;.docx&lt;/code&gt; file in Microsoft Word, they aren’t fighting broken floating textboxes or glued-together layouts—they get a clean, standard Word document styled with native typography.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  5. The Hard Reality: Document Fidelity Is an Ongoing Battle
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Does having a single data model magically solve every cross-format inconsistency? &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Honestly? No.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Word and web browsers use fundamentally different layout engines. Word calculates line wraps, margin collapse, and paragraph spacing under its own rules. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A layout that comfortably leaves 15mm of breathing room at the bottom of an A4 PDF might push the last bullet point onto page two in Microsoft Word. A header accent bar that looks sleek in CSS requires a careful table border hack in DOCX XML.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Maintaining parity between on-screen previews, generated PDFs, and native Word documents isn’t a one-and-done feature. It’s an ongoing process of calibrating margins, tuning line heights, and testing edge cases.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  6. What Building This Taught Us
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Building Pagewise forced us to look past the surface of web UI and think deeply about document architecture. &lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A resilient document editor has to separate three distinct layers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Content:&lt;/strong&gt; The raw data (your jobs, dates, skills, metrics).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Presentation:&lt;/strong&gt; How that data looks right now on the screen.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Target Output:&lt;/strong&gt; How that data must behave inside an external ecosystem (A4 paper, Adobe Acrobat, Word, ATS scanners).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When you keep those concerns clean and distinct, the system stops being a fragile hack and starts behaving like a real document engine.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  What's Next?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We're continuing to refine Pagewise’s rendering engine to make multi-page handling and layout customization even smoother. Alongside the document pipeline, we've also introduced &lt;strong&gt;Wiser AI&lt;/strong&gt; a companion tool that helps analyze job descriptions, flag missing skills, and suggest targeted resume improvements without taking away user control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want to see how the editor works in practice (or test the exports yourself), check it out here:&lt;br&gt;
👉 &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a href="https://pagewise.sadguruchenu.in/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pagewise&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h3&gt;


  Let's Chat
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you’ve built document editors, invoice generators, or report exporters:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How do you bridge the gap between browser DOM previews and print/export parity?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Do you use headless browser rendering, native PDF libraries, or something else entirely?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'd love to hear how other teams handle this in the comments!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The 3 AM Page: When Preview Chrome Becomes Render Artifact
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You wake to an alert: the automated video pipeline produced 47 corrupted exports overnight. Frame one shows your application's sidebar. Frame twelve captures a modal mid-transition. The encoder did exactly what you asked—it rendered the HTML you gave it, including the preview interface you forgot was part of the DOM tree.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the failure mode I am addressing while evaluating &lt;a href="https://github.com/heygen-com/hyperframes" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;heygen-com/hyperframes&lt;/a&gt;. Its premise, "Write HTML. Render video," makes HTML the production artifact. A preview dialog, responsive sidebar, or entrance animation must never quietly become part of that artifact.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a React application using Tailwind CSS v4 and Radix primitives, my recommendation is straightforward: &lt;strong&gt;isolate the render document, and animate only the surrounding interface&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  One Document, Two Consumers
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The architecture has three responsibilities.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The application produces a self-contained HTML document. The preview interface displays that document without modifying it. A render adapter submits the same document, together with explicit viewport and timing settings, to the video-rendering workflow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That adapter is where Hyperframes integration belongs. I am not assuming a particular repository command, rendering API, or timing contract here; those require verification against the version being adopted. The implementation below deliberately stops at the document boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not export the React application root. That couples video output to navigation, portal placement, loaded application styles, and whatever animation happens to be running when capture begins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Instead, treat the generated HTML as immutable input. Fonts, images, viewport dimensions, and the selected scene time are part of its rendering contract—not incidental browser state.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why the Tutorial Version Breaks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A common preview implementation measures a panel with &lt;code&gt;getBoundingClientRect()&lt;/code&gt;, writes a new width, then repeats that process on the next animation frame. The read after the write can force synchronous layout. Add text wrapping and image decoding, and a smooth-looking demo becomes a source of dropped preview frames and unstable measurements.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Moving the same animation into CSS does not automatically fix it: animating width or height still requires layout work. Use transforms and opacity for interface motion, and reserve fixed dimensions for the rendered document.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The related accessibility failure comes from treating a modal as an absolutely positioned &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;div&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt;. Adding &lt;code&gt;aria-modal="true"&lt;/code&gt; does not implement focus containment, Escape handling, or focus restoration. Keyboard navigation can continue behind the overlay while screen-reader users encounter a misleading dialog boundary.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Both failures come from giving preview chrome responsibilities it should not own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A Reusable Preview Boundary
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In an existing React/TypeScript application configured for Tailwind v4, install &lt;code&gt;@radix-ui/react-dialog&lt;/code&gt;. Save this component as &lt;code&gt;VideoPreview.tsx&lt;/code&gt; and import the stylesheet below through your application entry point.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;as&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Dialog&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"@radix-ui/react-dialog"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VideoPreviewProps&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="n"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VideoPreview&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;html&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;VideoPreviewProps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
